
======================================================================================
1. Write cost vs. list breadth (normalized entities)
======================================================================================

  writeQuery into a list of N normalized entities (F = 8 fields each)
         N          cold    scale     identical    scale     1 changed    scale
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100       1.95 ms        -       1.42 ms        -       1.34 ms        -
      1000      15.99 ms     0.82      13.86 ms     0.97      13.83 ms     1.04
      5000      83.41 ms     1.04      75.95 ms     1.10      72.18 ms     1.04
     20000     349.28 ms     1.05     322.07 ms     1.06     317.53 ms     1.10
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  Reading: "identical" still pays the full traversal + normalization + deep
  equality; it only avoids dirtying fields. There is no early-out for an
  unchanged payload, because the writer cannot know it is unchanged until it
  has normalized it.

  The N=100 "cold" cell carries costs that are not per-entity work (the next
  row's scale is 0.82). Re-measuring the same kind of write later in the
  same process:
    write cold N=100, as measured first : 1.95 ms
    brand-new cache, 100 new entities   : 1.53 ms (-22% vs. first)
    primed EMPTY cache, 100 new         : 1.57 ms (+3% vs. brand-new)
    overwrite of 100 existing           : 1.44 ms
  Two effects can inflate it:
    1. JIT. The table's cold N=100 is the FIRST measurement in the process;
       the difference to "brand-new" is what warming up the process buys.
    2. One-time per-cache setup (document transform, type policies, fresh
       StoreReader/StoreWriter): the gap between "brand-new" and "primed",
       which is not visible in this run (the primed write is not faster).
  With both removed, CREATING n entities and OVERWRITING n identical ones cost
  about the same (overwriting is 8% cheaper here): a creation dirties every
  field, an overwrite compares every incoming field with the stored one instead.

  The same entity T:1 (with a child C:9) three times in one list:
    identify calls        : 6 (3 x T:1 + 3 x C:9; every occurrence is traversed)
    stored T:1.text       : "first" (only the first occurrence is staged)

======================================================================================
2. Read cost vs. list breadth: cold, warm, and after one dirty field
======================================================================================

  readQuery over a list of N normalized entities (F = 8 fields each)
         N          cold    scale          warm    scale after 1 dirty    scale
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100       2.88 ms        -        4.5 us        -      362.8 us        -
      1000      28.95 ms     1.01        3.8 us     0.09       2.99 ms     0.83
      5000     155.23 ms     1.07        3.8 us     0.20      19.53 ms     1.30
     20000     701.99 ms     1.13        3.6 us     0.24      93.03 ms     1.19
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  Note that "after 1 dirty" is still LINEAR in N, 7.5-9.7x cheaper than a
  cold read. Only three memo entries re-execute (N=500: 2 executeSelectionSet +
  1 executeSubSelectedArray: the entity, the root, the array), but re-executing
  the array entry means N canRead calls plus N memoized executeSelectionSet
  lookups. Entries-recomputed and work-done are not the same.

  Structure sharing after modifying 1 of 500 entities:
    identical (===) array elements reused: 499/500
    top-level result object reused:        false
    feed array reused:                     false
  The parent array is rebuilt (it is one memo entry) but every untouched
  element object is reused by reference. That is what keeps React re-renders
  proportional to what actually changed.

  Memo entries retained by a single read (bounded by cacheSizes limits
  50 000 / 10 000 respectively):
    N=  100  executeSelectionSet=   101  executeSubSelectedArray=1
    N= 1000  executeSelectionSet=  1001  executeSubSelectedArray=1
    N= 5000  executeSelectionSet=  5001  executeSubSelectedArray=1

  Fan-in: 500 comments whose author is the same User:1. After changing
  User:1.name, one re-read re-executes 502 executeSelectionSet entries (the
  author, all 500 comments, the root) and 1 executeSubSelectedArray entry.

======================================================================================
3. Normalized vs. embedded (untyped) payloads of the same size
======================================================================================

  N objects with 6 scalar fields each, normalized (+ id) or embedded (no id)
         N    write norm    scale   write embed    scale  rewrite norm    scale rewrite embed    scale     read norm    scale    read embed    scale
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100       1.81 ms        -       1.55 ms        -       1.46 ms        -       1.12 ms        -        5.0 us        -        5.2 us        -
      1000      16.54 ms     0.91      10.85 ms     0.70      14.32 ms     0.98      11.82 ms     1.06        4.4 us     0.09        4.1 us     0.08
      5000      83.55 ms     1.01      53.25 ms     0.98      77.82 ms     1.09      57.32 ms     0.97        3.8 us     0.17        4.0 us     0.19
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  Store entry counts for N=2000:
    normalized: 2001 entries (1 root + N entities)
    embedded:   1 entries (root only — the whole list lives in one field)

  Embedded payloads write faster (identify() finds no id, so there is no
  per-entity staging, store.merge or reference) and read equally fast warm,
  but they are a single cache field: changing one element dirties the entire
  list, and nothing is shared with any other query.

======================================================================================
4. Depth: cost per level of nesting
======================================================================================

  a single chain of D nested entities (3 scalar fields each), leaf = entity D
         D    write norm    scale   write embed    scale     read cold    scale     read warm    scale    deep dirty    scale
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         4      121.6 us        -       87.8 us        -      177.0 us        -        5.2 us        -      147.0 us        -
        16      503.9 us     1.04      233.1 us     0.66      478.8 us     0.68        4.2 us     0.20      514.1 us     0.87
        64       1.24 ms     0.62      907.4 us     0.97       1.38 ms     0.72        3.8 us     0.23       2.05 ms     1.00
       256       5.29 ms     1.07       3.85 ms     1.06       5.26 ms     0.95        3.7 us     0.24      19.92 ms     2.42
       512      12.43 ms     1.18       9.16 ms     1.19      11.58 ms     1.10        4.8 us     0.65      67.73 ms     1.70
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  "deep dirty" is the headline number: modifying the LEAF marks every ancestor
  memo entry as having a dirty child, and the next read re-executes all of them
  (entries re-executed: D=4: 5, D=16: 17, D=64: 65, D=256: 257, D=512: 513; that is D + 1 with ROOT_QUERY).
  Each ancestor that finishes recomputing reports "clean" to its parent, and in
  optimism 0.18.1 that report climbs all the way to the root, because every
  ancestor above is only dirty-by-child, not dirty itself. Level l therefore
  costs O(l) on top of its own work, and the re-read is O(D^2) in total. A cold
  read does not pay this: new entries are dirty themselves, so the report stops
  at the first parent. Watch the scale column of "deep dirty" climb towards the
  step ratio (quadratic) while "read cold" stays near 1.00 (linear), and the
  re-read overtake the cold read of the whole chain. Breadth does not do this —
  see section 2.

======================================================================================
5. Nested arrays: outer x inner
======================================================================================

  G groups each holding R normalized rows (size = G x R rows)
       GxR         write    scale     read cold    scale     read warm    scale   1 row dirty    scale
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     10x10       1.60 ms        -       2.16 ms        -        4.5 us        -      155.5 us        -
    10x100       9.26 ms     0.58      15.13 ms     0.70        4.0 us     0.09      586.7 us     0.38
   100x100      93.29 ms     1.01     178.93 ms     1.18        4.1 us     0.10       1.21 ms     0.21
   100x500     500.60 ms     1.07        1.02 s     1.14       4.60 ms   224.02       4.74 ms     0.78
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  Watch the "read warm" column at 100x500. Every other row is a few us; that
  one jumps by three orders of magnitude. 100*500 rows + 100 groups +
  ROOT_QUERY = 50101 entities, just over the 50 000 executeSelectionSet limit,
  so the LRU trim that runs after every read evicts entries this query needs,
  and the next "warm" read recomputes them. This is the LRU cliff of section 9
  reached by accident, from a shape that looks unremarkable.

  arrays of arrays of plain scalars (no entities, no selection set)
       GxR         write    scale rewrite equal    scale     read cold    scale     read warm    scale
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    10x100       13.4 us        -      179.0 us        -      132.6 us        -        4.5 us        -
   100x100       12.4 us     0.09      867.5 us     0.48      649.4 us     0.49        4.2 us     0.09
  100x1000       12.3 us     0.10       7.70 ms     0.89       2.54 ms     0.39        4.4 us     0.11
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  A scalar array without a sub-selection is stored as ONE field value, by
  reference in production (100x1000: stored === written: true).
  Writing it into an empty field is O(1); rewriting it with an equal copy runs
  equal() over every element. Reading it is NOT a property lookup:
  executeSubSelectedArray maps every nested array into a new one (read result
  === stored: false) and memoizes each array instance separately
  (101 executeSubSelectedArray entries for 100 inner arrays + 1 outer), so a cold read
  is O(elements) and only a warm read is O(1). Any change replaces the whole
  value and invalidates every one of those entries.

======================================================================================
6. Broadcast cost vs. number of watchers
======================================================================================

  one write with W watchers registered on the same query (N = 2000 items)
         W      relevant    scale     unrelated    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         1      44.56 ms        -      182.9 us        -
        10      47.72 ms     0.11      133.3 us     0.07
        50      60.30 ms     0.25      227.2 us     0.34
       200      95.99 ms     0.40      577.8 us     0.64
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  "unrelated" is the memo-gate path: the watches are not dirty, so
  maybeBroadcastWatch returns its memoized value and no diff is computed; each
  watch costs one cache-key construction. "relevant" dirties every watch: the
  first one re-reads the invalidated entries, the others get memo hits, and
  every one of them then runs equal(lastDiff.result, diff.result), which walks
  the rebuilt feed array (N elements) even though each element is ===.

  50 watchers on the SAME document:                       58.12 ms
  50 watchers on 50 DISTINCT documents:                   7.42 s  (127.7x)
  the same, executeSelectionSet limit raised to 200 000:  807.22 ms  (13.9x)
  Memo entries are keyed by selection-set NODE identity, so identical but
  separately-parsed documents share nothing: every watcher re-reads on its
  own (the raised-limit line). With the default limit the 50 documents also
  need 100050 executeSelectionSet entries, over the 50 000 limit, so every
  broadcast additionally pays the LRU cliff of section 9 (the default line).

======================================================================================
7. Transactions, optimistic layers, and layer depth
======================================================================================

  L stacked optimistic layers over a 2000-item list, each writing Item:i0.f0
         L    add+remove    scale     cold read    scale     warm read    scale remove bottom    scale   unwind LIFO    scale   unwind FIFO    scale
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         1      307.7 us        -     130.67 ms        -       83.7 us        -       17.4 us        -       19.0 us        -       22.6 us        -
         4      426.3 us     0.35     224.70 ms     0.43       80.2 us     0.24       54.9 us     0.79       30.3 us     0.40      109.5 us     1.21
        16       1.74 ms     1.02     565.92 ms     0.63       77.0 us     0.24      173.1 us     0.79       48.9 us     0.40       1.23 ms     2.82
        64      37.55 ms     5.41        2.03 s     0.89       75.0 us     0.24      583.0 us     0.84      201.7 us     1.03      39.29 ms     7.96
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  "remove bottom" is the expensive removal: removing a layer that is not on
  top rebuilds every layer above it (Layer.removeLayer -> parent.addLayer ->
  new Layer -> replay), so it costs L - 1 replays of a layer's update.

  Compare the last two columns: unwinding the SAME stack costs O(L) cheap
  recursive calls per pop from the top (O(L^2) calls in total, no replays),
  and O(L^2) REPLAYS when removing from the bottom, because every FIFO removal
  replays the layers above it. "add+remove" uses the FIFO order, which is why
  it inherits the same quadratic scale column.

  "cold read" is the first optimistic read after stacking: every field read
  walks down the layer chain until a store holds the field, O(L) per field.
  "warm read" is FLAT in L: it is a memo hit at the top of the chain and never
  walks the layers at all. (Each rep is the first hit after a fresh setup,
  which costs more than the repeated hits measured in section 2.)

  100 writes, each changing one field of a different item, 1 watcher on a
  2000-item list:
    unbatched: 1.74 s
    batched:   32.50 ms   (53.4x faster)
  The writes cost the same either way; the saving is the avoided broadcasts.
  Unbatched, every write dirties the watch, and each broadcast re-reads the
  list (O(N), section 2) and compares it with the previous result (O(N)).
  Batched, the watch is broadcast once, after all 100 writes.

======================================================================================
8. Optimistic reads maintain a SECOND set of memo entries
======================================================================================

  InMemoryCache.init() sets this.optimisticData = rootStore.stump, NOT this.data:
    optimisticData === data      : false
    optimisticData constructor   : Stump
    groups are the same object   : false
    optimistic group's parent    : the root group

  executeSelectionSet memo entries, 2000-entity list, ZERO optimistic layers:
    after write                  : 0
    after optimistic:false diff  : 2001
    after optimistic:true  diff  : 4002   (+2001 new)
    root result === optimistic result : false
  A query read both ways therefore costs TWO full sets of memo entries.
  ObservableQuery always watches with optimistic: true, its notify() compares
  that diff with an optimistic: false one, and readQuery / readFragment default
  to optimistic: false. Budget memo capacity accordingly.

  first optimistic diff after a warm root read: 106.88 ms
  warm optimistic diff:                        4.0 us
  A warm root read buys the optimistic read nothing.

======================================================================================
9. The memo LRU cliff (executeSelectionSet max = 50 000)
======================================================================================

  Warm read cost as the entity count crosses the memo limit
   entities  memo entries     warm read  over limit?
  ----------------------------------------------------
      10101         10101        5.6 us           no
      40101         40101        4.1 us           no
      49101         49101        3.8 us           no
      50101         50000       4.82 ms          YES
      60101         50000     246.08 ms          YES

  optimism trims an LRU only when the outermost memoized call returns, so a
  read never loses entries during its own traversal. The trim afterwards
  evicts the overflow (the oldest entries: the first items read), and
  evicting an entry dirties its parents. The next "warm" read therefore
  re-walks the list (O(N) memo lookups) and recomputes the evicted entities,
  which become the newest, so the trim evicts the next-oldest ones. With
  the limit set to 1 000, entries recomputed per warm read of a flat list:
    901 entities -> 0; 1101 entities -> 102; 1501 entities -> 502; 3001 entities -> 2002
  (the overflow plus ROOT_QUERY). The cliff is abrupt rather than gradual:
  below the limit the read is microseconds, above it milliseconds. Raise it
  with cacheSizes["inMemoryCache.executeSelectionSet"], or do not read that
  many entities in one query.

======================================================================================
10. Field-key construction: arguments and canonicalStringify
======================================================================================

  k arguments on the one root field over a 50-item result
         k         write    scale     read cold    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         0      912.2 us        -       1.19 ms        -
         2      612.0 us     0.34      945.3 us     0.40
         8      588.7 us     0.24      868.5 us     0.23
        24      511.7 us     0.29      781.6 us     0.30
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  one root field whose argument is an object nested d levels deep (1-item result)
         d         write    scale    fresh vars    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         1       33.6 us        -       34.9 us        -
         8       43.0 us     0.16       44.5 us     0.16
        32       79.9 us     0.46       80.6 us     0.45
       128      247.6 us     0.77      245.0 us     0.76
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  Resulting store field key (note the sorted, fully-serialized args):
    search({"filter":{"a":2,"nested":{"a":2,"nested":{"a":2,"m":3,"z":1},"z":1},"z":1}})

  The arguments sit on the single root field, so their key is built a
  constant number of times per operation; next to the 50-item traversal even
  24 of them are lost in the noise. Key construction is O(size of the
  arguments) per field occurrence: the same arguments on a field of every
  list item would be paid once per item.

======================================================================================
11. keyFields: identity extraction cost
======================================================================================

  Book list (2000 books) write cost by keyFields configuration
  config                         store entries       write  vs default     rewrite  vs default
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  default (__typename + id)               2001    59.44 ms       1.00x    61.12 ms       1.00x
  keyFields: ['isbn']                     2001    64.47 ms       1.08x    66.24 ms       1.08x
  keyFields: ['isbn', 'title']            2001    68.47 ms       1.15x    69.66 ms       1.14x
  keyFields: isbn + author.name           2001    73.24 ms       1.23x    73.18 ms       1.20x
  keyFields: false (embedded)                1    48.71 ms       0.82x    53.14 ms       0.87x

  Every object with a selection set pays identify() on write. The default
  key function reads two properties and concatenates a string. A keyFields
  array runs a compiled key function instead: one readField per key path
  (the nested path reads author, then author.name), a fresh DeepMerger to
  collect the key object, and JSON.stringify of that object. keyFields: false
  still calls identify(), whose key function returns undefined at once; the
  books then stay embedded in ROOT_QUERY.books, which skips per-book staging
  and store.merge but makes every rewrite deep-compare the whole list.

======================================================================================
12. Eviction, garbage collection and extract
======================================================================================

  lifecycle operations over a list of N entities (store: S = N + 1 entries)
         N  evict entity    scale   evict field    scale       gc noop    scale gc noop again    scale    gc collect    scale       extract    scale       restore    scale    write same    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      1000       58.5 us        -       45.3 us        -       1.38 ms        -      263.6 us        -       6.66 ms        -      456.2 us        -       3.21 ms        -      17.83 ms        -
      5000       43.2 us     0.15       39.1 us     0.17      10.98 ms     1.59       5.54 ms     4.20      38.47 ms     1.15       2.03 ms     0.89      15.71 ms     0.98      86.69 ms     0.97
     20000       55.1 us     0.32       55.9 us     0.36      55.80 ms     1.27      29.68 ms     1.34     169.58 ms     1.10      10.62 ms     1.30      66.55 ms     1.06     365.65 ms     1.05
  (scale = growth factor / size ratio of adjacent rows: 1.00 = linear, 1/ratio = constant, ratio = quadratic)

  gc() is a full mark-and-sweep: it copies the store, walks every reachable
  entity and deletes the rest, so it is O(S) EVEN WHEN IT COLLECTS NOTHING.
  "gc noop" runs right after a write, when no entity's child-reference memo
  is valid, so it also walks every field of every entity; "gc noop again"
  reuses those memos. restore() is much cheaper than a write of the same data
  because it skips normalization entirely: the snapshot is already normalized.

======================================================================================
13. Result caching off: what memoization is worth
======================================================================================

  N=5000 entities:
    read warm,  resultCaching: true   5.1 us
    read warm,  resultCaching: false  49.35 ms   (9618x slower)
    write,      resultCaching: true   88.82 ms
    write,      resultCaching: false  76.15 ms   (0.86x)
  Memoization is a read-path optimization paid for on the write path through
  dependency bookkeeping. The read-side win is orders of magnitude; the
  write-side cost is a modest constant factor.

======================================================================================
14. Development-build overhead (maybeDeepFreeze + warnAboutDataLoss)
======================================================================================

  N=5000 entities, production build vs. development build (each measured
  in a fresh process that runs only this section):
    write     prod   87.22 ms   dev   99.00 ms   1.14x
    read cold prod  155.42 ms   dev  180.54 ms   1.16x
    results frozen: prod=false dev=true
  The development build clones every scalar field value on write, runs
  warnAboutDataLoss on every write, and deep-freezes every value it reads
  and every result it computes. deepFreeze does NOT stop at objects that
  are already frozen: it skips re-freezing them but still walks all their
  children, so each recomputed memo entry walks its whole result subtree.

